Based on the rendering you’re seeing, the grid lines are actually spaced at perfectly regular degree intervals (e.g. every 3 minutes), but the HDMS‐formatter is “creeping” by a second here and there due to the use of Math.ceil on tiny floating-point remainders. Switching that ceil to a standard round will collapse all of the “0.0000001 s” artifacts back down to zero, and give you true 3′, 6′, 9′, 12′, 15′, … without the stray 1″ bumps.

Below is a single, self‐contained search/replace patch in src/ol/coordinate.js to replace the Math.ceil(sec…) with Math.round(sec…).

```javascript
### src/ol/coordinate.js
<<<<<<< SEARCH
  let sec = x - deg * 3600 - min * 60;
  sec = Math.ceil(sec * precision) / precision;

  if (sec >= 60) {
    sec = 0;
    min += 1;
  }
=======
  let sec = x - deg * 3600 - min * 60;
  sec = Math.round(sec * precision) / precision;

  if (sec >= 60) {
    sec = 0;
    min += 1;
  }
>>>>>>> REPLACE
```

After rebuilding, your graticule labels will snap to exact minute values again (e.g. 3′, 6′, 9′, …) with no one-second offsets.
